[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Gaetani et al., 2019-09)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/157a25bd7a9cb5bff789a977bac58a0d9154f7ff)
- venue: Journal of Proteome Research
- citation count: 253
[method]
The paper used 1D PISA, a single-temperature proteome-wide thermal shift assay, to measure compound-dependent changes in soluble protein abundance. In the experimental sections, K562 cells were treated with MTX, 5-FU, or a panel of 9 small-molecule drugs, subjected to a single heat step in the 48-58°C thermal window, centrifuged to separate soluble and insoluble proteins, and the soluble fraction was quantified by TMT-based LC-MS/MS. The paper also simulated 1D PISA outputs from existing TPP melting curves to evaluate whether a single PISA measurement could retain target-engagement information. This method is directly related to the proposed plan's cell-based PISA platform, although the paper's described validation was cell-based and did not include the native lysate arm or the 96-compound TMTPro 16-plex screen.
[results]
The 1D PISA assays recovered known target engagement: MTX and 5-FU produced thermal stabilization of their annotated targets, DHFR for MTX and TYMS for 5-FU, and the 9-drug panel generated compound-specific thermal-stability hits consistent with the annotated targets of the tested compounds. The simulation of 1D PISA from TPP data showed that the single-temperature PISA output preserved target-engagement signals while reducing the number of required thermal measurements, supporting the proposed high-throughput PISA workflow and the 8-fold throughput advantage relative to full TPP. The available excerpt does not provide numeric fold changes, p-values, or exact hit counts, so no further quantitative outcomes can be reported.
[context]
Key conditions indicated by the paper sections were K562 cells, a 48-58°C thermal window, a single heat step for 1D PISA, vehicle controls, TMT multiplexing, and LC-MS/MS quantification. The 9-drug panel comprised compounds with well-annotated targets, including kinase inhibitors, and was used to validate target engagement and specificity. The provided excerpt does not state exact heat temperature, drug concentrations, treatment duration, or TMT plex size; the proposed plan specifies 10 μM for 30 min, 48-58°C, TMTPro 16-plex, and hit criteria of |log2FC| > 0.2 and >3.5 SD from protein-specific variance.
[end related work 1]

