- venue: Scientific Reports
- citation count: 41
[method]
The paper used a targeted cellular thermal shift assay, CETSA, to assess binding of stapled peptides to Mdm2 and Mdm4 in cells. Cells were treated with stapled peptides, subjected to thermal denaturation, and soluble Mdm2/Mdm4 abundance was measured after centrifugation. This is directly related to the proposed K562 cell-based thermal profiling method because both infer compound-protein engagement from increased soluble protein stability after heat treatment, but the paper used a targeted CETSA readout for Mdm2/Mdm4 rather than the proposed proteome-wide PISA workflow with TMT-based mass spectrometry.
[results]
The available section headings report that stapled peptides bound Mdm2 and Mdm4 at high affinity in CETSA and that modifications of the stapled peptides were evaluated for their effect on Mdm2/Mdm4 binding. No quantitative fold changes, temperature values, IC50 values, or statistical significance values are provided in the supplied text.
[context]
The supplied excerpt does not specify the cell line, peptide concentrations, thermal gradient range, centrifugation parameters, or quantification instrument. The only method-specific context is that CETSA was performed following modification of stapled peptides to assess Mdm2/Mdm4 binding. This provides a targeted cell-based thermal shift precedent for the research plan, which extends the concept to K562 cells, a 48 to 58 degree Celsius thermal window, and proteome-wide soluble protein quantification.
[end related work 1]

[start related work 2]
[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Massey, 2018-04)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/d968a6dca045ce64533cc039429052e79fe514c6)
- venue: PLoS ONE
- citation count: 27
[method]
The paper used a targeted cell-based thermal shift assay, described as CETSA by immunofluorescence (HCIF-CETSA), in HT29 cells to assess Chk1 thermostabilization after compound treatment. Cells were treated with V158411 or other Chk1 inhibitors, optionally in the presence of hydroxyurea, then heated in an imaging-compatible 96-well plate using either immersion in pre-heated PBS or a sealed low-well-volume heating method. After heating, cells were fixed and analyzed by single-cell immunofluorescence for nuclear Chk1 signal. This relates to the proposed K562 PISA cell-based thermal profiling because both use compound treatment followed by controlled thermal denaturation to infer protein engagement, but the paper method is a single-target immunofluorescence CETSA rather than a proteome-wide soluble-fraction TMT/MS PISA assay.
[results]
The supplied excerpt does not provide numeric fold changes, melting-temperature shifts, or p-values. It reports that V158411 did not increase Chk1 thermostabilization in intact cells after immersion in pre-heated PBS. It also reports that, under sealed low-well-volume heating, V158411 decreased Chk1 nuclear immunofluorescence in HT29 cells, and that structurally distinct Chk1 inhibitors similarly decreased Chk1 nuclear fluorescence after low-well-volume heating. Hydroxyurea-induced Chk1 activation was examined as a condition affecting Chk1 thermostabilization by V158411, but the excerpt does not specify the direction or magnitude of that effect.
[context]
Key conditions include HT29 cells, an imaging-compatible 96-well plate, low-well-volume sealed heating versus immersion in pre-heated PBS, Chk1 nuclear immunofluorescence as the readout, and treatment with V158411, other Chk1 inhibitors, and hydroxyurea. The excerpt does not specify exact temperatures, heating durations, compound concentrations, antibody dilutions, or imaging instrument settings. The relevance to the research plan is that it addresses cell-based thermal profiling and heating control in 96-well plates, but it does not use the proposed 48-58°C PISA soluble-proteomics workflow.
[end related work 2]

