  "suggested_action": "The most important improvement is to add a rigorous pilot validation of the K562 thermal profiling protocol before the full 96-compound screen, using known direct binders and non-binders to test whether the proposed 48-58°C window, 10 μM 30-minute treatment, centrifugation, and TMT-based mass spectrometry reliably detect direct engagement. This should be informed by [(Massey, 2018-04)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/d968a6dca045ce64533cc039429052e79fe514c6), which shows that heating method can change the direction of the thermal shift signal, and by [(Tan et al., 2015-07)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/0de8ce01c8cc0c9446d52e9c8f457891718f7b9f), which provides a targeted CETSA precedent for Mdm2/Mdm4 engagement. The pilot should compare multiple heating conditions, including sealed low-volume heating, immersion in pre-heated buffer, and rapid equilibration, and should include vehicle controls, heat-only controls, compound-only controls, lysate-only controls, and known negative compounds to distinguish true direct binding from heating artifacts, secondary cellular effects, or centrifugation-related solubility changes. The next most important action is to strengthen the distinction between direct and indirect engagement by pairing the proposed cell-based PISA assay with lysate-based PISA, targeted CETSA, dose-response experiments, biochemical binding assays, and western blotting, as already outlined in the research plan. This is especially important because the cell-based K562 assay will capture signaling, protein-protein interaction, and post-translational modification effects, while the lysate assay should enrich for direct ligand binding. The proposed statistical criteria, log2 fold changes greater than 0.2 in absolute value and changes greater than 3.5 standard deviations from protein-specific variance, should be validated against known binders and non-binders before being applied to the full library, and the TMTPro 16-plex design, approximately 384 hours of instrument time, and the expected scale of approximately 871,120 cell-based and 627,176 lysate-based measurements should be accompanied by explicit quality-control metrics for normalization, plex effects, missing values, and replicate agreement. The least important but still useful action is to treat [(Xi et al., 2019-10)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/92d6201de32fcbd130c5617d3c4f2fba3109691e) only as weak conceptual support, because the supplied text lacks protocol, quantitative, and statistical details needed to justify the proposed method.",
